table records the requested and achieved sample sizes, \code{all_rows_used},
whether the precision target was met, and whether the ordinate passed the
independent numerical-stability gates. Finite row budgets use a reproducible
simple random sample
without replacement. For a purely continuous ordinate, \code{relative_mcse} and
\code{ess} describe the selected continuous posterior-row sequence. For a mixed
point/continuous ordinate evaluated on every active continuous row, they use
the full conditioned-chain sequence, with zero contributions for inactive
rows, and therefore include active-mass uncertainty and its covariance with
continuous contributions. With a subset of active rows,
\code{active_branch_relative_mcse} describes the selected active-row contribution
sequence and \code{active_mass_relative_mcse} comes from the complete binary
indicator chain. \code{relative_mcse} is their conservative worst-correlation
delta upper bound on the unconditional density scale. It is suitable as a
descriptive precision diagnostic but does not certify the unobserved
full-mixture covariance, so \code{bf_grade_met} remains false.
\code{sampling_relative_mcse} separately reports the finite-population
simple-random-sampling component conditional on the fitted rows
(\code{sampling_uncertainty_type = "finite_population_srswor"}). The sampling
design is exact, but the standard error is an estimate: the usual
without-replacement formula applied to the sample variance of the selected
contributions, and zero when every eligible row is used. It is not
added to the selected-row sequence MCSE, which already describes the actual
selected estimator sequence.

These fields concern the continuous density ordinate. At a location that is
also a posterior atom, the atom probability remains a separate discrete mass
and is never added to the density ordinate.

\code{ordinate} and \code{log_ordinate} describe the posterior density at the requested
point, not the model's marginal likelihood. \code{log_ordinate} is retained only
when actually computed; it is not reconstructed for older results.
\code{numerical_status} distinguishes \code{finite}, \code{underflow}, \code{overflow},
\code{arithmetic_failure}, \code{invalid_density}, \code{zero_without_log_evidence},
\code{nonfinite}, and \code{not_computed}. Underflow/overflow requires finite log
evidence and confirmation that exponentiating that log loses range. A zero
without this evidence is not classified as a mathematical zero. A small
density alone does not establish unreliability.

Failed requested points retain separate diagnostic records with a
\code{failure_reason}, including failures occurring before a final ordinate was
obtained. \code{status = "unavailable"} distinguishes these failures from a
finite positive ordinate rejected by accuracy diagnostics. Ordinary-scale
precision diagnostics lost to underflow/overflow are unavailable, not zero
error or proof of convergence. Retaining a log estimate does not authorize a
Bayes factor: the existing finite-positive ordinate requirement is unchanged.
Legacy tables remain inspectable with unknown new fields set to \code{NA}.
A hypothesis result without attached diagnostics, such as one whose point
ordinates were estimated with \code{density_method = "KDE"} or one without a
point hypothesis, stops with the class
\code{RoBMA_density_diagnostics_unavailable}.
Density ordinate, diagnostic-unavailable, and plot conditions share the
\code{RoBMA_density_error} parent while retaining their specific condition classes.

The reliability policy warns when relative MCSE is at least 5 percent, ESS is
below 100, the largest contribution share is at least 20 percent, or fewer
than 100 finite contributions remain. These same-sample diagnostics do not
reject a finite fixed-design estimate. Their warning thresholds are returned
as columns rather than being implicit. For a descriptive mixed density curve
using the conservative partial-row bound, a finite ESS below the usual curve
rejection threshold is retained as a warning when the quantitative
mixture-scale MCSE checks pass.

qCMDE's full method diagnostics distinguish \code{pilot_normalization_integral}
from \code{final_normalization_integral}; IWMDE uses
\code{support_grid_normalization_integral}. This compact accessor reports the
relative difference of the final integral from the active mass as
\code{normalization_relative_error}. For qCMDE it is a bookkeeping check, not a
discretization error: rows normalized on the lattice are normalized by the
same trapezoid rule, on the same grid, that integrates them, so they sum to
the active mass up to roundoff by construction, and rows normalized in
closed form contribute their analytic mass within the range. The
discretization and truncation errors of the lattice normalizers are reported
by \code{ordinate_relative_change}, \code{lattice_resolution_relative_error}, and
\code{truncation_ordinate_bound}. The active
policy check is identified by \code{stability_metric}: qCMDE checks its posterior
ordinate error estimate (\code{ordinate_error_estimate}), whereas IWMDE checks
normalization mass. The qCMDE estimate adds three parts. \code{ordinate_relative_change}
